The right answer doesn't get any mass under the posterior!

Pedantry aside, the means I got aren't too unreasonable: they're .08
and 1.07 for mu and sigma. The ranges are a little misleading because
the distribution is skewed. Here are quantiles:

> quantile(temp5$mu)

        0%        25%        50%        75%       100%

-0.5246780 -0.1060650  0.0391880  0.2226787  2.6395300

> quantile(temp5$sigma)

      0%      25%      50%      75%     100%

0.817321 0.996989 1.060570 1.133740 1.785260

Matt

On Wed, Sep 5, 2012 at 4:43 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
> But doesn't that mean (sorry again for
> all the puns) that the posterior mean of
> mu is above 0.5 and the posterior mean for
> sigma is around 1.3?  That's close to what
> I got:
>
>
>  quantile(tns$mu)
>
>          0%        25%        50%        75%       100%
>  -0.1016810  0.5404598  0.8620630  1.2935150  3.7038100
>
>  quantile(tns$sigma)
>
>        0%      25%      50%      75%     100%
>  0.961492 1.225290 1.332955 1.457465 1.974080
>
> The "right" answer is mu=0 and sigma=1,
> which don't get much mass in the posterior!
>
> This is another good example where the MAP estimate
> (which is around mu=0 and sigma=1 according to our
> model) is pretty far from the posterior mean on the
> scale we care about.
>
> - Bob
>

