n 9/12/12 11:07 PM, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> He later said that it was g++-4.4.x on RedHat. But, test-unit still works for me. Anyway, the end of autocovariance.hpp
> looks messed up.

Indeed it is.  Thanks for the heads up.

See below for the fix.

>
>      template <typename T>
>      void autocovariance(const std::vector<T>& y,
>                          std::vector<T>& acov,
>                          Eigen::FFT<T>& fft) {
>
>        stan::prob::autocorrelation(y, acov, fft);
>
>        T var = stan::math::variance(y) * (y.size()-1) / y.size();
>        for (size_t i = 0; i < y.size(); i++) {
>          acov[i] *= var;
>        }
>      }
>
>      template <typename T>
>      void autocovariance(const std::vector<T>& y,
>                          std::vector<T>& acov) {
>        Eigen::FFT<T> fft;
>        return autocovariance(y,acov,fft);
>      }
>
> Since the second version doesn't have a Eigen::FFT argument like the first version does, I think it shouldn't have
> return type void.

I just patched it by removing "return".

The return type of void was right, but there shouldn't
have been a return statement.  All this function does is
allocate the FFT object and then pass it to three-argument
version that fills in the acov vector passed by reference.
The three-argument version is there to allow you to reuse
the FFT object across multiple calls (it does some internal
allocation that I think can be reused, though I haven't
done speed tests).

- Bob

