MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I patched the bug in type inference for constrained
vectors (simplex, ordered) and matrices (covariance
matrices, correlation matrices).  The fix was
pushed to Google code in commit:

   commit 5179786a67fd53a1f995bd2131eeb50e69b1fd9d
   Author: Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com>
   Date:   Tue Sep 18 15:24:03 2012 -0400

   fixed issue with hidden dims for corr_matrix, cov_matrix, simplex, and ordered

Until our next official release, putting any of these constrained
vector or matrix types in an array makes them invisible to the code.
There's not a good workaround because the constraints are necessary.

If you want to work with the above commit tag, it's good
to go from Git.

It was another "C++ isn't like Java" bug that bit me.
I redeclared member variables on the constrained subclasses
of matrix types, but called them through superclass refs,
so the correct ones were hidden.  I'm sort of surprised
lint didn't catch that -- the Java compiler issues a warning
if you hide variables like this.

Thanks again for the report and sorry again for the bug.

- Bob

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Tue Sep 18 20:22:22 2012
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Tue, 18 Sep 2012 20:22:22 +0000
Subject: 2nd & 3rd derivative auto-dif for diagonal RM-HMC
Message-ID: <q7ol5CEmsKY.D4lQGAbVqF4J@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/q7ol5CEmsKY/m/D4lQGAbVqF4J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

We discussed using auto-dif at the meeting today
for second- and third-order derivatives.  The
chain rule's a bit messier and also involves the
lower-order derivatives:

   http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain_rule#Higher_derivatives

The derivative propagation can still be done in one
efficient backward pass one edge of the graph at a time.

So we'd need to extend the auto-dif vars to have
values, first derivatives, second derivatives, and
third derivatives.  So it'll take about twice as much
space.  One approach would be to write chain1(), chain2()
and chain3() derivative propagation rules, running each
in turn (each is required for the next).  These can then
be consolidated into chain() which just calls them in order.

The nice part is that it won't mess with vectorization
in our probabilty functions.

The not-so-nice part is that it requires new auto-dif
variables (easy) and a whole lot of chainN() method writing
(a whole lot of slogging).

- Bob

From u1004dd6f66bb10c3 at groups.google.com  Tue Sep 18 21:09:50 2012
